  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 237 PM EDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nToday-Friday... Florida will remain situated between two upper level \nhigh pressure systems, one in the Atlantic and one over TX/SW US, as \nan upper level trough shifts eastward towards the eastern US. \nShortwave energy will continue to traverse the base of this trough \nover the Deep South and across the Florida peninsula. At the \nsurface, high pressure across the Atlantic will slowly shift \nnorthward, with the axis remaining over Florida. Locally, offshore \n(S-SW) flow will persist with speeds 5-10 mph each day. The east \ncoast sea breeze is forecast to form each afternoon, turning the \nwinds onshore (E-SE) as the sea breeze pushes inland. Moisture will \nremain near to slightly above normal, with PW values 1.8-2.3\" each \nday. This will support a medium to high (50-70 percent) chance of \nshowers and storms through the period. Scattered showers and storms \nare pushing in across the Treasure Coast as well as into Lake County \nfrom the south and west this afternoon. The east coast sea breeze \nhas begun to push onshore this afternoon, mainly from Cape Canaveral \nsouthward, with scattered storms forming along the sea breeze. CAM \nguidance shows additional storms developing along the I-95 corridor \nin the mid-afternoon as the sea breeze moves inland, as well as \nshowers and storms forming across the interior by late afternoon and \nevening. Similar pattern is forecast for Friday. Highest coverage \nwill be across the interior each afternoon and evening where the sea \nbreeze and outflow boundary collisions are forecast to occur. \n\nThe environment supports storms each day with plenty of instability \n(MUCAPE 1800-2400 J/kg), ample buoyancy (lifted index of -4 to -7), \nadequate downdraft potential (DCAPE 700-900 J/kg), and somewhat \ncooler temperatures aloft (-7C at 500 mb). Main storm hazards will \nbe frequent lightning, gusty winds of 45-55 mph, small hail, and \nheavy rainfall of 1-3 \" which may lead to minor localized flooding \nin urban and poor drainage areas. Any lingering activity will \ndiminish around midnight, with activity across the Atlantic waters \ncontinuing through the overnight hours. Temperatures will be near to \nslightly above normal, with afternoon highs in the low to mid 90s \nwith peak heat indices of 100-106F. Overnight lows will be in the\nlow to mid 70s. \n\nThe National Hurricane Center (NHC) has upgraded AL96 to Tropical \nStorm Dolly in the central tropical Atlantic today. Conditions \nremain unfavorable for strengthening over the next 5 days. There are \ncurrently no specific concerns to east central Florida at this time. \nResidents and visitors should continue to monitor the forecast for \nupdates. \n\nSaturday-Thursday... Upper level high pressure over the Atlantic and \nthe SW US will merge over the weekend and remain over the southern \nUS/Gulf/Atlantic into early next week. At the surface, high pressure \nover the Atlantic will continue to shift northward towards the \nCarolinas into early next week. A frontal boundary will slide \nsouthward into the Deep South/Northern Florida this weekend before \nstalling, eventually becoming diffuse by early next week. Locally, \nwinds will shift onshore (east to southeast) this weekend and remain \nthrough at least mid next week as the surface high pressure moves \nnorthward of Florida. Moisture across the local area will remain \nnear to above normal, with PW values of 1.8-2.1\". Daily afternoon \nshowers and lightning storms are forecast to continue, with \nscattered storms forming each day along the advancing east coast sea \nbreeze, with additional showers and storms forming across the \ninterior. Highest coverage of storms will be across the interior, \nwhere the sea breeze collision is forecast to occur each evening. \nStorm hazards will be similar to what they are today through late \nweek. Temperatures will remain seasonably hot and humid with \novernight lows continuing to be warm and muggy.",
